/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The efficiency so found is called the air-standard efficiency.
   - Since T₂/T₁ = T₃/T₄ = rγ−1, the efficiency 1 − qout/qin simplifies to:
   - r is the compression ratio and γ = 1.4 for air.
   - With the pressure ratio α = p₃/p₂, the mean effective pressure is mep = p₁ r (rγ−1 − 1)(α − 1)/[(γ − 1)(r − 1)].
   - The Diesel cycle adds heat at constant pressure, 2→3, while the piston moves out; heat addition stops at the cut-off ratio ρ = v₃/v₂.
   - ηDiesel = 1 − (1/rγ−1) × (ργ − 1)/[γ(ρ − 1)]
   - The bracket (ργ − 1)/[γ(ρ − 1)] is greater than 1 for every ρ > 1, so at the same r the Diesel cycle is less efficient than the Otto cycle, and it gets worse as the cut-off ratio grows (more fuel, more load).
   - The dual (limited-pressure) cycle adds heat first at constant volume, 2→3, with α = p₃/p₂, and then at constant pressure, 3→4, with the cut-off ratio ρ = v₄/v₃; expansion is 4→5 and rejection 5→1.
   - ηdual = 1 − (1/rγ−1) × (αργ − 1)/[(α − 1) + γα(ρ − 1)]
   - Put ρ = 1 and it becomes the Otto formula; put α = 1 and it becomes the Diesel formula.
*/
