/* book Worked example 2: Q: A Diesel cycle has a compression ratio of 16 and a cut-off ratio of 2, starting from the same 1 bar and 300 K (cp = 1.005 kJ/kg·K). Find the peak pressure, the corner temperatures, the efficiency and the mep, and compare with the Otto cycle of the first example.
   1. State 2: 160.4 = 3.031, so T₂ = 909.4 K; 161.4 = 48.50, so p₂ = p₃ = 48.5 bar. This is the peak pressure.
   2. State 3: T₃ = ρ T₂ = 2 × 909.4 = 1818.9 K.
   3. State 4: T₄ = T₃ (ρ/r)0.4 = 1818.9 × (2/16)0.4 = 1818.9 × 0.4353 = 791.7 K.
   4. qin = 1.005 × (1818.9 − 909.4) = 914.0 kJ/kg; qout = 0.718 × (791.7 − 300) = 353.0 kJ/kg; w = 561.0 kJ/kg.
   5. Efficiency: 561.0/914.0 = 0.614. By the formula: bracket (21.4 − 1)/(1.4 × 1) = 1.639/1.4 = 1.171, and η = 1 − 0.3299 × 1.171 = 61.4 %.
   6. mep: v₂ = 0.861/16 = 0.0538 m³/kg, so mep = 561.0/(0.861 − 0.0538) = 695 kPa = 6.95 bar.
   7. Comparison: peak pressures 48.5 and 48.0 bar, nearly equal, but the Diesel cycle gives 61.4 % against 56.5 % and more work per kg (561 against 450 kJ). At equal r = 16 an Otto cycle would give 67.0 %, so the order flips with what is held equal.
   Answer: Peak pressure 48.5 bar; T₂ = 909 K, T₃ = 1819 K, T₄ = 792 K; η = 61.4 %, mep = 6.95 bar. At nearly the same peak pressure the Diesel cycle beats the Otto cycle by 4.9 points. */
