/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The fuel-air cycle keeps the air-standard processes (isentropic compression and expansion, instantaneous constant-volume burning in the Otto form, no heat loss, no friction) but uses the real working fluid.
   - The result is that the fuel-air cycle efficiency is well below the air-standard value at the same compression ratio; for a chemically correct petrol mixture it is typically about four fifths of it.
   - The mixture strength is measured by the equivalence ratio φ, the actual fuel-air ratio divided by the chemically correct one (φ = 1 correct, φ > 1 rich, φ < 1 lean).
   - This is why an SI engine is set slightly rich for full power and leaner for cruising economy.
   - An indicator diagram records cylinder pressure against volume in a running engine.
   - With indicated work Wi (net area of the diagram), brake work Wb and fuel energy Qf per cycle: ηi = Wi/Qf, ηm = Wb/Wi and ηb = Wb/Qf = ηi ηm.
*/
